---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import RhythmCompare from "../../components/Learn/RhythmCompare.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="vertical-rhythm-round" topic="rhythm">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A unitless <code>line-height</code> multiplies the font size, so a 29px heading
      at <code>1.2</code> gets a 34.8px line. Every heading size lands somewhere different,
      and text drifts off any grid you lay under it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keeping it on a grid meant a Sass function per size, and that breaks as
      soon as sizes are fluid. Not anymore.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>round(up, value, step)</code> rounds a length up to a multiple of <code
          >step</code
        > at computed time, whatever the value is
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>1em + 2 * var(--ui-rhythm-step)</code> is two steps of leading: tight
        for large headings and roomy for small ones, rounded up so it never clips
      </li>
      <li>Font sizes snap to half a step, so fluid sizes move in steps</li>
      <li>
        Margins and flow space round to the same <code>--ui-rhythm-step</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>@property</code> makes the flow space a length, so <code
          >1.25em</code
        >
        resolves against the body text, not each heading
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`h1, h2, h3 {
  line-height: round(up, 1em + 2 * var(--ui-rhythm-step), var(--ui-rhythm-step));
}

h2 {
  font-size: round(var(--ui-font-size-h2), var(--ui-rhythm-step) / 2);
}

@property --flow-space {
  inherits: true;
  initial-value: 0px;
  syntax: "<length>";
}

.rich-text {
  --flow-space: round(1.25em, var(--ui-rhythm-step));
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Drag the font size. The lines mark every step, and the shaded bands are
      the line boxes.
    </p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <RhythmCompare />
    </Example>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>1.2</code> lands the line box wherever the multiplication ends, so most
        sizes stop between two lines and the drift adds up with every line
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>round()</code> rounds each line box up to the next step, so every line
        box ends on the grid
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The real theme on the same grid. Each heading and paragraph ends on one of
      the lines.
    </p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <div class="rhythm-demo ui-rich-text">
        <h2 class="ui-h2">A heading that wraps onto two lines</h2>
        <p>Body text sets the flow space for everything around it.</p>
        <h3 class="ui-h3">A smaller heading</h3>
        <p>Text that follows the heading it belongs to.</p>
      </div>
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>round()</code> is Baseline since 2024, so this works in every current
        browser
      </li>
      <li>
        There's no <code>@supports</code> fallback, and every <code
          >round()</code
        > takes a custom property, so an older browser can't drop the declaration
        while parsing
      </li>
      <li>
        It becomes invalid at computed-value time instead: <code>font-size</code
        > and <code>line-height</code> inherit from the parent, and margins fall back
        to zero
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>

<style>
  .rhythm-demo {
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      oklch(from var(--ui-primary) l c h / 35%) var(--ui-border-width),
      transparent var(--ui-border-width)
    );
    background-size: 100% var(--ui-rhythm-step);
    inline-size: min(100%, 28rem);
  }
</style>
